SELECT
    'scan_inflation_swaps_extremes_tool',
    'inflation_swaps',
    'scanner',
    '{}'::jsonb,
    $tref$Fabozzi & Mann (eds.), Handbook of Fixed Income Securities, 9th ed., McGraw-Hill, Ch. 50-51 (Inflation-Linked Derivatives and the Inflation Swap Market) — textbook framing of the ZCIS quoted-rate level as the market's required inflation compensation under a specific index family, with cross-curve comparability subject to index-family and market-structure caveats. Diebold & Rudebusch (2013), Yield Curve Modeling and Forecasting, Princeton University Press, Ch. 3-4 — statistical framing of rolling z-score level-extreme screens against own-history distributions, applicable to any rate series including inflation-derivatives par rates. ISDA 2006 Definitions, Inflation Derivatives Supplement (Annex) — canonical conventions for ZCIS index references and per-curve indexation lag / interpolation (USD_ZCIS: 3M lag, Daily interpolation, CPI-U NSA; EUR_ZCIS: 3M lag, Monthly, HICPxT; GBP_ZCIS: 2M lag, Monthly, RPI). Bloomberg Inflation Derivatives documentation (USSWIT, EUSWI, BPSWIT tickers) — per-pillar quoted-rate observation source (PX_MID Bloomberg mnemonic). Methodology guardrail per the primitive catalog (build_order 25, inflation_swaps__scan_inflation_swaps_extremes): "Methodology card must state the z-score lookback window explicitly" — honoured here via the verbatim per-row and per-response methodology_disclosure. The 252-day rolling z-score, ddof=1, 60-observation minimum, 1.5x calendar-day buffer multiplier mirror the sibling sovereign / OIS / linker / bond_futures scanners; convention values are pinned by the cross-config lint per docs_revamped/03_standards/methodology_disclosure.md.$tref$,
    $klim$INDEX-FAMILY MISMATCH (load-bearing): USD_ZCIS references US CPI-U NSA (CPURNSA Index), EUR_ZCIS references Eurozone HICP ex-tobacco (CPTFEMU Index), GBP_ZCIS references UK RPI (UKRPI Index). Ranking ZCIS-rate z-scores across this heterogeneity is a useful morning screen but a 2.5σ extreme on USD_ZCIS 10Y reads against a different underlying inflation-compensation distribution than a 2.5σ extreme on GBP_ZCIS 10Y; the desk reader MUST interpret the ranking under that caveat, and the per-row underlying_index field surfaces the exact Bloomberg index ticker the contract references. MARKET-STRUCTURE MISMATCH (load-bearing): ZCIS markets differ across currencies in index-publication lag (USD_ZCIS 3M / EUR_ZCIS 3M / GBP_ZCIS 2M), interpolation convention (USD Daily vs EUR/GBP Monthly), dealer-quote liquidity, and structural basis vs the bond-implied breakeven curve. A z-score extreme on one market may reflect liquidity / structural-basis moves rather than pure inflation-expectations divergence. SINGLE-METRIC RANKING by ZCIS rate LEVEL z-score per the catalog's literal wording (required_metrics: [yield_mid]); daily_change_bps and monthly_change_bps are surfaced as per-row snapshot context but are NOT separate rankings (multi-metric expansion would widen scope past the catalog entry). MORNING SCREEN, NOT a tactical trade signal — the scan answers "where is the ZCIS curve stretched today?" and surfaces extremes for further analysis via inflation_swap_curve_spread / inflation_swap_forward / inflation_swap_butterfly / cross_market_inflation_swap_spread / swap_breakeven_basis_simple. Closed-family whitelist: only USD_ZCIS / EUR_ZCIS / GBP_ZCIS are accepted (sourced from the inflation_swap_curve_families YAML convention); passing a nominal sovereign / linker / OIS / policy-futures curve_family is refused at schema-validation time with routing guidance. The fetch window is METHODOLOGY (z_score_window_days × z_score_buffer_multiplier = 252 × 1.5 = 378 calendar days) — NOT